- Add tests to tests/unit_parse.py and tests/unit_py_evaluation.py.  These should cover every construct that could be encountered in ICurry.
- fix tests in unit_py_constraints.py
- fix tests in unit_py_pulltab.py
- Come to think of it, I should remove the mutual recursion between D, N, and S.  All of the recusion should be done synthetically.
    - Until then, RuntimeState::stack_exhausted guards the C stack: hnf and
      procN return E_UNWIND at the stack_limit flag, procD rotates the queue,
      and a configuration that cannot proceed within the limit raises a
      "stack limit" error.  A configuration that needs more nesting than the
      limit allows cannot finish; only an explicit evaluation stack fixes that.
- curry.compile seems unable to import Control.SetFunctions
- Documentation:
    - Add information about debugging the C++ backend.
        - Keep the testcase very small.  Reduce it if necessary.
        - Build a unit test and then start the `debug_tests` script.
        - Break at `procD` to get started.
        - Use the gdb command `tty` to direct trace output to another terminal.
        - Use the `str` method to inspect values:
            - Node *:   gdb> p node->str()
            - Cursor:   gdb> p cursor.str()
            - Variable: gdb> p variable.str()
        - To inspect an inductive position relative to the root of a possible
          redex, use the version of `Cursor::str` that takes a variable.  For
          example:

              Cursor redex;
              Variable inductive;
              gdb> p redex.str(inductive);
              head (_a:_b)
                    ~~~~~
                    path: [0], guards: {}

        - By default, `str` substitutes free variables with stylized names _a,
          _b, _c, ...  This can make it difficult to trace free variables
          through steps, as shown below:

              (head (_a:_b), _c)  ->  (_a, _b)

          The variable _c here apparently morphs into _b in the second
          expression.  Pass PLAIN_FREEVARS to any of the `str` methods to avoid
          this.  Free variables will instead be shown with their numeric ID,
          which is fixed.  Using this, the above example might be rendered as
          follows:

              (head (_0:_1), _2)  ->  (_0, _2)




2026-10-02
- readFile on a missing file raises different errors on the two backends: the
  Python backend raises Python's FileNotFoundError from open ("[Errno 2] No
  such file or directory: 'nofile'"), the C++ backend raises an
  EvaluationError ("i/o error: No such file or directory: nofile").  Recorded
  only; the parity gap is not fixed.
- The C++ backend did not report non-determinism in monadic actions.  hnf
  pull-tabbed a choice at the inductive position of any step, so putChar
  ('a' ? 'b') forked and printed both characters, where the Python backend
  raised NondetMonadError.  Only writeFile and appendFile checked for the
  pull-tab and raised the error, with a text of their own ("nondet error:
  file.txt").  Fixed; see the entry below.
- The Python graph copier (backends/py/graph/copy.py) recursed once per node,
  so a cyclic expression raised RecursionError when it was copied, and so did
  a long list value.  Fixed: the copier keeps its own stack and records the
  copy of a node before its successors, as the C++ copier does, so sharing
  and cycles are kept.  The graph equality (equality.py) had the same
  recursion and got the same fix.  Tests: unit_py_copy.py, unit_equality.py.
- Python backend performance.  The evaluator spent most of its time in
  bookkeeping, not in rewrite steps: isinstance checks against abstract base
  classes (inspect.is_boxed, inspect.isa_unboxed_primitive), the visitation
  dispatch in graph/indexing.py, the construction of Variable objects, trace
  bookkeeping that ran with tracing off, and a generator-based context
  manager entered once per step.  Fixed with per-type caches for the ABC
  checks (validated with abc.get_cache_token), one flat loop for indexing,
  __slots__ on Node and Variable, a plain context manager for catch_control,
  and no-op trace helpers when tracing is off.  No semantic change and no new
  flag.  Evaluation time, median of three runs: SomeNum.check 300, 4.49 s to
  1.35 s; naive reverse of 400 elements, 4.02 s to 0.87 s;
  benchmarks/Multgen, 18.40 s to 4.96 s.  The assert in
  Variable.from_logicalpath that indexed the path a second time was removed;
  unit_indexing cross-checks every realpath result.  Tests: unit_py_graph.py,
  unit_py_control.py, unit_inspect.py, unit_indexing.py.
- Non-determinism in monadic actions on the C++ backend (see above).  Fixed:
  hnf reports the error when the redex is monadic (is_monadic), or when
  ($!), ($!!), or ($##) evaluates the argument of a monadic function, as the
  Python _applyspecial does.  The IO built-ins in io.cpp carry F_MONADIC,
  like the generic Prelude table.  Both backends raise "non-determinism in
  monadic actions occurred!".  The text is defined once per backend
  (NONDET_MONAD_ERROR_TEXT in cyrt/builtins.hpp, exceptions.NondetMonadError)
  and unit_io_bind ties them together.  writeFile and appendFile lost their
  own check.  Found on the way: putChar ('a' ? 'b') crashed the C++ backend,
  because ($!) read its argument variable again after hnf had forwarded the
  redex; ($!!) and ($##) with a choice argument failed the source != target
  assertion in pull_tab, because procN scanned the choice as its root (hnf
  runs first now).  The C++ catch applied the handler to the action instead
  of the error value and never cleared the error, so a handler that read
  its argument, or raised an error of its own, aborted the process.  Now
  catch pops the error and hands the IOError value to the handler; an error
  without a value (Prelude.error) becomes IOError msg, as with the PAKCS
  catch.  The C++ error and error2 steps no longer quote the message.  The
  rule still differs between the backends: the Python backend keys it on a
  list of Prelude names (rts_control.is_io) and raises the error on any
  unwind in those steps; the C++ backend reacts to a choice in any monadic
  step, so a user function that calls putStrLn and evaluates a choice is an
  error there and a fork on the Python backend.  Tests: unit_io_bind.py,
  unit_prelude.py.
- C++ backend compile time.  g++ took about 1.0 s for a one-line module
  (-O3; 0.65 s of it in the front end, for cyrt/cyrt.hpp), and PAKCS with
  icurry 4.9 s when the .icy was not cached.  func_eqconstr recompiles its
  767 modules after every libcyrt rebuild, about 770 s of g++.  Measured: a
  lower optimization level saves nothing without a precompiled header (-O0
  0.92 s, -O3 0.98 s) and costs run time (Tak1 and PermSort: -O1 10 to 55
  percent slower, -O0 2.3 to 5 times slower, -O2 equal to -O3); the jq pass
  costs 3 ms per module and 0.09 s on the Prelude, the same as the internal
  encoder.  A precompiled header halves the g++ time (0.45 to 0.5 s per
  module).  Done: Cpp2So precompiles cyrt/cyrt.hpp once per flavor into
  <include>/cyrt/cyrt.hpp.gch/ (SPRITE_CXX_PCH_ROOT relocates or disables
  it) and builds it again when libcyrt.so or a header is newer; make
  uninstall removes the directory.  func_eqconstr on the C++ backend with
  every .so recompiled: 920 s before (as reported), 552 s after; one chunk
  of 143 modules, forced recompile: 165 s before, 117 s after.  The time
  that remains per module is template instantiation for the static data
  (the Metadata maps 0.18 s, the ModuleBOM 0.25 s, the step functions
  0.05 s); extern template declarations do not help, only another
  representation of the module data would.  The Prelude takes 28 s at -O3
  (17 s at -O1, 5 s at -O0), with or without the precompiled header.
  Tests: unit_cxx_toolchain.py.
- C++ collector.  The collector never ran before one billion nodes existed,
  because the nodes Python holds (values handed to Python, expressions built
  with curry.expr) were not roots.  Done: a Node wrapper registers its node
  as a root for as long as it lives (RootHolder in the bindings; gc_add_root
  and gc_remove_root in graph/memory.cpp).  The mark phase also visits the
  error value of a configuration and the queue of every SetEval node it
  meets, which holds the alternatives a lazy set function has not produced
  yet; the old mark phase missed both.  A collection inside a nested
  evaluation (a set function, a step run from Python, an evaluation started
  in a Python callback) sweeps only the nodes allocated since that
  evaluation began and treats the older nodes as roots, because the steps
  of the enclosing evaluation hold nodes in C++ locals (EvaluationScope in
  procD and in the step binding).  The mark phase points a forward node at
  the end of its chain, as compress_fwd_chain does; without that, a
  forwarded root kept every redex of a tail recursion alive.  Dead nodes go
  to free lists by size instead of free(): with free(), the sweep cost
  30 ns per node, and PermSort ran 75 percent slower after its first
  collection, because the evaluation reused scattered chunks from malloc.
  The default threshold is 2^20 nodes; SPRITE_GC_THRESHOLD overrides it,
  and the adaptive policy never shrinks below it (the policy changed; see
  the review follow-ups below).  Measured on the C++ backend with the
  former doubling policy, collector off / at 2^20, evaluation time and the
  peak resident set of the process (VmHWM; the figures given here before
  were VmPeak, the peak virtual size; times vary by 15 percent between
  runs): Tak1 8.8 / 7.2 s and 3.27 GB / 115 MB; Queens10 11.9 / 6.2 s and
  5.20 GB / 115 MB; PermSort 9.3 / 12.5 s and 4.87 / 3.21 GB; ReverseHO
  2.2 / 2.0 s and 1.15 GB / 655 MB; QueensSet9 6.0 / 8.7 s and 3.16 /
  3.16 GB.  2^16, 2^18, and 2^22 are within the noise of 2^20; 2^24 is
  slower on every program (measured with the doubling policy).  A walk over a million list cells (1.5 GB of short-lived
  nodes) completes under a 1 GiB address-space cap, outside and inside a
  set function; with the old threshold both runs end in MemoryError.  The
  process maps 97 MB at load instead of 577 MB (the address vector was
  reserved for the old threshold).  Bindings: gc_collect, gc_node_count,
  gc_root_count, gc_num_roots, gc_collections, gc_seconds, gc_threshold,
  gc_set_threshold, gc_eval_depth.  Tests: unit_cxx_gc.py; the
  out-of-memory program in CxxRuntime.curry now keeps its list reachable.
  Left: the runtime never deletes a Configuration, a Queue, or a Set (the
  3 GB of PermSort and QueensSet9 are mostly those), and the free-variable
  table keeps every free variable and its generator alive, so a program
  that narrows many variables keeps them all.  A raw Arg object held by
  Python is not a root (make_node creates them only for the duration of a
  call).  A set function whose value is the guarded argument itself hits
  assert(0) in procD (T_SETGRD at a configuration root); seen while the
  test program was written, not fixed.  gc/mps.cpp does not define the new
  entry points.  A collection inside a nested evaluation pushes and traces
  every older node, so it costs the whole heap and reclaims nothing from
  before the nested evaluation began (see the header of wdgc.cpp); the
  precise fix is to register the Variable and Cursor locals of a suspended
  step as roots.
- Characters.  The C++ backend stored a Char as a signed byte: ord (chr
  160) was -96, a character above U+00FF could not exist, and a non-ASCII
  character could not be passed from Python (the Arg caster took one byte).
  The Python backend kept code points, but read a file byte by byte, and
  could not compile a string literal with a non-ASCII character (it emitted
  a bytes literal).  Done: a Char is a Unicode code point on both backends.
  unboxed_char_type is char32_t; the Char node keeps its 16 bytes.  Text
  crosses the boundary of the runtime as UTF-8 (cyrt/utf8.hpp): the string
  literals of generated code (_dquote spells the UTF-8 bytes with octal
  escapes; a character literal is a char32_t literal such as U'\xe4'),
  build_curry_string and extract_string, readFile, writeFile, appendFile,
  putChar, getChar, and the display of a cstring node.  A malformed byte
  sequence in a file or on stdin becomes U+FFFD, as in PAKCS.  show writes
  every code point outside printable ASCII as a decimal escape of at least
  two digits ('\228', '\00'), as PAKCS does; the Python show did so below
  256 only.  The literal readers accept every code point (the bound was
  256), and the Python reader accepts uppercase hex digits.  The Python
  backend keeps a string literal as a memoryview of 4-byte code points
  (prelude.string.codepoints), so _biString still slices in O(1).  Found on
  the way: the C++ table of a literal case (ValueSet) held one byte per
  Char, and the runtime read it with an Arg stride, so a free variable at a
  case over two or more character literals read out of bounds; the table
  holds Args now.  ValueBindingsMaker split an odd-sized table as size/2
  and size/2, so a case over three literals lost one value.
  Variable::operator[] dropped the realpath of its parent, so writeFile,
  which head-normalizes the head of its string through such a variable, ran
  the step of the wrong node when the character was not evaluated yet:
  writeFile f (map chr xs) crashed the C++ backend.  The ICurry lexer read
  at most three digits of a decimal escape, so '\128512' became four
  characters.  Tests: unit_chars.py, unit_prelude.py, unit_io_bind.py,
  unit_py_conversions.py, readshow/unicode.curry, io/readFile_utf8.curry,
  io/writeFile_utf8.curry.  Left: the standard streams of the Python
  backend keep the encoding of sys.stdin and sys.stdout (UTF-8 under a
  UTF-8 locale, which the test drivers set).  A lone surrogate from chr is
  written as three bytes by the C++ backend and rejected by the Python
  writeFile.  topython joins a list of strings into one string, so a [Char]
  or [String] value arrives as str; not changed.
- Review follow-ups on the entries above.
  Collector policy: after a collection the threshold is 8 times the
  survivors, but not less than the configured floor, instead of one
  doubling per collection when 15 percent survived.  The heap stays within
  8 times the live nodes (the former bound was about 13).  Measured on the
  C++ backend, former policy / growth 8 / collector off, with the
  evaluation time, the number of collections, the time in the collector,
  and VmHWM: Tak1 7.2 / 6.9 / 8.8 s, 59 / 59 / 0 collections, 115 MB /
  115 MB / 3.27 GB; Queens10 6.2 / 6.4 / 11.9 s, 97 / 97 / 0, 115 MB /
  115 MB / 5.20 GB; PermSort 12.5 / 13.0 / 9.3 s, 8 / 11 / 0 collections,
  0.8 / 1.0 / 0 s in the collector, 3.21 / 3.12 / 4.87 GB; ReverseHO 2.0 /
  2.1 / 2.2 s, 4 / 2 / 0, 655 MB / 839 MB / 1.15 GB; QueensSet9 8.7 / 6.7 /
  6.0 s, 5 / 2 / 0 collections, 2.6 / 0.7 / 0 s in the collector, 3.16 GB
  in all three.  Growth 2 and 4 were slower on PermSort (19.6 and 15.9 s)
  and on QueensSet9 (8.8 and 8.2 s).  On PermSort the time is not in the
  marks, which take 1 s of the 13 s, but in the evaluation after a sweep,
  which reuses the dead nodes in scattered order; returning the dead nodes
  in allocation order did not help (13.5 s).  PermSort and QueensSet9 stay
  slower with the collector than without it because the runtime never
  deletes a Configuration, a Queue, or a Set (the entry above).  That leak
  is the prerequisite for a further gain; the benchmark README notes the
  two programs.  Test: unit_cxx_gc.py.
  Stale C++ caches: the C++ emitter stamps every generated file with its
  format (// FORMAT: 2, compiler.FORMAT_VERSION), and the toolchain starts
  again from the JSON file when a cached .cpp carries another stamp or none
  (Cpp2So.is_stale, Plan.prune_stale).  Before this, a .cpp written by the
  old emitter and newer than its .json.z was compiled against the new
  runtime, which read the one-byte-per-Char table of a literal case with an
  Arg stride.  A .so without its .cpp is still trusted by its timestamp.
  The successor caster maps a Char above U+10FFFF (prim_chr does not check
  its argument; chr clamps) to U+FFFD instead of raising ValueError.  Tests:
  unit_cxx_toolchain.py, unit_plan.py, unit_chars.py.  Found on the way,
  not fixed: the C++ emitter must not run for a module whose shared library
  is loaded in the process.  vIsBuiltin asks the runtime module for the
  symbol, and a loaded library registers every symbol it defines, so the
  emitter declares them all extern and defines none; the library compiled
  from that file fails to load with an undefined symbol.  A first, wrong
  version of the stamp reader did this to the installed Prelude after a
  curry.reload in a test process (the regenerated Prelude.cpp had 0
  definitions and 90 extern declarations).  SPRITE_FORCE_RECOMPILE_CXX
  with a curry.reload can do the same.
  Python copier: an unboxed successor (an int, a float, a char, the
  memoryview of a string literal, the iterator of a generator node) is
  shared, not deep-copied.  copy.deepcopy rejects a memoryview, so a
  partial application that held a string literal could not become a value.
  Tests: unit_py_copy.py, unit_prelude.py (both backends).
  UTF-8 decoder: a sequence cut short, by the end of the input or by a byte
  that cannot continue it, counts as one malformed sequence, and the second
  byte is checked against the range its lead allows.  So both backends give
  the count of U+FFFD that the 'replace' handler of Python gives; the C++
  decoder gave one per byte.  getChar still drops the bytes it read after
  such a lead.  Tests: unit_chars.py.
  inspect caches: the per-type caches of is_boxed and isa_unboxed_primitive
  decide from the type alone and fall back to isinstance for an object
  whose __class__ differs from its type (a proxy, a mock).  Test:
  unit_inspect.py.
  ICurry lexer: the empty escape (backslash, ampersand) is accepted, and the
  seven-digit decimal escape has unit tests (unit_parse.py).
  Tests: unit_io_bind.py and unit_chars.py evaluate the goals of
  data/curry/IOBind.curry and data/curry/Chars.curry, built once, instead
  of compiling a module or an expression per test.  cytest.run_in_subprocess
  sets PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 for its children, and func_io compares the
  UTF-8 file as bytes, so neither depends on the locale.  The precompiled
  header has a test for a failed build.
  Monadic non-determinism, a gap on both backends: a choice met by a
  Curry-defined combinator before any monadic step is pull-tabbed to the
  top, so mapM_ putChar ("ab" ? "cd") prints abcd and yields two values.
  PAKCS prints abcd and then reports the non-determinism error.  Recorded
  as an expected failure (unit_io_bind.py, test_mapM_nondet_is_an_error).

2026-10-03: defects found while writing the examples (not fixed)
- curry.save(module, file) without goal= writes a footer that runs nothing
  and ignores -g on the command line; the saved program prints nothing where
  sprite-exec prints the value. examples/04-static-compile is a known failure
  in tests/unit_examples.py for this reason.
- C++ backend: a set function that chooses among sub-terms of its own
  argument (set1 anyOf xs, set1 tails xs, set1 id x, set1 (\xs -> head xs ?
  last xs)) hits an assertion in procD or hnf (fairscheme.cpp) and aborts.
  Choices built from fresh data work. The Python backend accepts every shape.
  Fixed on 2026-10-04 (the set guard at the root, below).
- A free variable created in Python (curry.free()) inside a goal gives a wrong
  value for xs ++ [3] =:= [1,2,3] &> xs, and two of them crash both backends.
  Free variables declared in Curry work.
- show of a Float differs between the backends: "3.5" on the Python backend,
  "3.5000000000000000" on the C++ backend.
- Picking distinct items from a shrinking pool in a recursion (let d = anyOf
  pool in f (delete d pool) (d : acc)) emits its values and then aborts on the
  C++ backend and does not end on the Python backend; structural permutations
  (ndinsert) work.
- Residuation-style arithmetic over free Int variables (the sendMoreMoney
  program of the test data) suspends on the C++ backend and times out on the
  Python backend.
- inspect.getimpl raises ValueError on the C++ backend, and the per-function
  ICurry view prints only "exempt" for some functions (examples/03-inspect).
- A goal without a type signature can come back to Python unevaluated as a
  partial application when PAKCS gives it a class-constrained type.

2026-10-03
- Stale C++ caches (issue #15).  currentfile dropped a .so that was older
  than libcyrt.so (by ctime), so every make stage that relinked the library
  invalidated every object, and the first import after it compiled the
  Prelude again (about 30 s).  Done: each object gets an ABI stamp, a file
  beside it (<module>.so.abi) that holds a digest of the installed runtime
  headers (cxx.toolchain.runtime_digest: SHA-256 over the names and the
  contents of the headers under include/cyrt, 16 hex digits, cached per
  process).  Cpp2So writes the stamp after g++ succeeds and refuses an
  object whose stamp is missing or differs from the digest of the installed
  headers; the plan then compiles the .cpp file again (Cpp2So.is_stale,
  Plan.is_stale, Plan.prune_stale).  The last stage of a plan has no step,
  so the step that made its file answers for it.  The digest leaves out the
  build id of libcyrt.so on purpose: a relink after a .cpp-only change keeps
  the ABI of generated code, and the headers are that ABI.  The source chain
  (.curry, .icy, .json.z, .cpp) keeps the filesys.newer rule.  The
  precompiled header compares its age with the headers only, not with
  libcyrt.so.  SPRITE_IGNORE_CYRT_TIMESTAMP is gone; SPRITE_FORCE_RECOMPILE_CXX
  stays.  Format stamp: the reader scans the first 16 lines and finds the
  stamp on line 3 of a generated file (section comment, import list,
  stamp), so the hazard of the latency analysis (a stamp that is never read
  makes every process compile everything again) was not in the committed
  tree.  Cpp2So now checks its input and raises CompileError when the stamp
  does not read back, so that hazard is an error at the first compile, not
  a silent recompile in every process.  Measured on the C++ backend: a
  second process that imports a module compiled by an earlier process runs
  zero g++ commands (counted through a hook on _system.pexec), the Prelude
  included; the Prelude import costs 0.04 s of CPU.  A make stage that
  changes nothing relinks and copies libcyrt.so (the archive rule touches
  libcyrt.a), which invalidated every object under the old rule; now the
  Prelude object, its stamp, and the precompiled header stay, and the next
  process runs zero g++ commands.  One added comment line in a header,
  followed by make stage, drops the Prelude object and keeps its .cpp file;
  the next import runs two g++ commands (the header member and Prelude.so),
  and the process after it none.  An installation from
  before this change holds a Prelude.so without a stamp, which is compiled
  once more (its Prelude.cpp is kept).  Tests: unit_cxx_toolchain.py
  (TestAbiStamp, the stamp-reader tests), unit_findcurry.py, unit_plan.py.
  Left: a change to the set of builtin symbols in the runtime (a .cpp-only
  change under currylib) changes what the emitter writes for the Prelude,
  which neither stamp sees; raise FORMAT_VERSION for such a change.  A
  comment-only edit of a header invalidates every object; there is no
  switch to trust the objects anyway.
- jq removed (issue #16).  The ICurry-JSON encoder (icurry.json.Encoder)
  writes the compact form itself: no space after a comma or a colon, every
  character outside ASCII escaped.  That is the output of the jq pass
  (--ascii-output --compact-output) byte for byte, and the converter appends
  the newline that jq wrote, so a cache from before this change equals a
  cache from after it.  Gone: config.jq_tool, the --with-jq option and the
  JQ_EXECUTABLE setting of configure, the jq package and option in the CI
  workflow, the pipe and input arguments of _system.pexec, and the external
  icurry2jsontext path of the converter (dead since 2021).  Found on the
  way: IModule stored its imports as a set, so the order of the import list
  in the JSON followed the hash seed of the process, and a fresh conversion
  matched a committed cache only by luck of the seed.  The imports now keep
  the order of the ICurry file (duplicates dropped).  Checked against the
  eight library caches under curry/pakcs-3.4.1: seven were in file order
  already; Data/List.json.z held ["Data.Maybe","Prelude"] and was
  regenerated; all eight now match a fresh conversion under any hash seed,
  the compressed bytes included.  Measured: the conversion of the Prelude
  costs 1.75 s of CPU, almost all in the reader; the jq child cost 0.08 to
  0.09 s of CPU on the Prelude and about 3 ms per small module.  Tests:
  unit_parse.py (EncodeJSON: the compact form, the import order, every
  library cache against the encoder), unit_make.py.  Left:
  src/export/tools/jq.link and the jq filter in src/export/tools/Makefile
  can go.  With no JQ_EXECUTABLE in Make.config the link is skipped at
  install time; the filter never removed it, because Make.rules expands
  LINKS before the filter runs.
- Prebuild in make stage (issue #17).  make stage installed only the .curry,
  .icy, and .json.z files of the Curry library, so the first import after a
  stage generated and compiled the Prelude: about 30 s on the C++ backend;
  and on the Python backend every process compiled the 1.2 MB Prelude.py
  from source (0.2 s), because the loader ran the file through runpy and
  the test drivers run Python with -B.  Done: the library root Makefile
  (curry/Makefile) has a prebuild step that runs after src and the library
