- Ownership of configurations, queues, and sets (G1, issue #25).  The C++
  runtime never freed a Configuration, a Queue, or a Set: drop popped the
  front configuration and leaked it, fork leaked the parent of its two
  clones, and the queues and sets of set functions came from new without
  delete.  PermSort and QueensSet9 held about 3 GB, mostly those objects.
  Done: a Queue owns its configurations (state/queue.hpp): pop_front lets
  the front one go, the destructor the rest, and the last queue to hold a
  configuration destroys it (Configuration::holders).  The split of a set
  function's queue on an escaping choice (allValues_step) put one
  configuration object into both queues and let both step it; now both
  queues hold it, and a queue clones it before it steps it
  (Queue::unshare_front, from ready()), so neither sees the steps of the
  other.  The runtime state owns its outermost queue.  The queue of a set
  function belongs to its SetEval node, but a copy of the node shares the
  queue, so the collector frees queues and sets: both register themselves
  (graph/memory.cpp); the mark phase marks the queues on the stacks of the
  runtime states and the queues of the SetEval nodes it reaches, and the
  sets of those queues, of those nodes, and of the SetGuard nodes it
  reaches; the sweep destroys the rest.  A pointer read from a node counts
  only when the registry knows it, because a guard built from Python
  carries a number in the set slot.  allValues_step pops the nested queue
  by RAII, so an error inside a set function leaves the stack of queues as
  it was.  The trace keys its tables by a serial number of the queue, not
  by its address.  Configurations come from a free list, and the scan keeps
  its levels and its call stack in SmallVecs (inline room 4 and 6; SmallVec
  gained resize), so a clone makes no heap call; sizeof(Configuration) is
  376 bytes, in one block.  Found on the way: every collection of
  QueensSet9 ran inside a nested scheduler, where every older node is a
  root, so it marked the whole heap, freed nothing (100 percent survived),
  and raised the threshold to eight times the heap.  A nested scheduler now
  hands E_GC outward as it hands E_ROTATE, and the outermost scheduler of
  the state collects with no step of the state on the C stack; only an
  evaluation started from a Python callback still collects nested.  The
  live configurations also request a collection when they reach an eighth
  of the node threshold, with the same growth rule, because a set function
  consumed only in part (isEmpty) leaves its queue to the collector while
  few nodes are allocated.  SPRITE_GC_GROWTH sets the growth factor
  (default 8).  Bindings: gc_configuration_count, gc_queue_count,
  gc_set_count, gc_queue_lengths, gc_growth; a debug build asserts after
  every collection that the live configurations are exactly those in the
  registered queues, with their holders.  Measured with the harness (C++
  backend; memory suite, one repetition, peak RSS and CPU seconds, old to
  new): PermSort 3036 MB to 466 MB and 10.41 s to 7.73 s (steps equal);
  QueensSet9 3073 MB to 391 MB and 6.82 s to 7.59 s (steps 12140753 to
  12143159 with the collector on, 12141274 to 12144098 with it off; forks
  2979434 to 2978907 and 2979262 to 2979174).  The split changes the
  counters of every program with a choice that escapes a set function:
  before, both queues stepped one configuration object, so a choice made in
  one queue was made for the other; now each clone makes its own choices.
  QueensSet of the throughput suite is such a program: 2172616 steps and
  511344 forks in the baseline, 2152081 and 511413 after G1 and T3
  together (alias inlining changes the steps, the split alone the forks).
  The collection points changed the schedule as well until G2 (below)
  stopped the rotation after a collection: since then QueensSet9 counts
  12144098 steps and 2979174 forks with the collector on and off alike
  (memory suite, measured on 2026-10-04).  Throughput suite, three
  repetitions, CPU seconds: Tak1 2.76 to
  2.45, Fib 0.80 to 0.74, Queens10 4.19 to 3.90, PermSort 10.48 to 7.63,
  Reverse 1.00 to 0.96, Primes 5.97 to 5.52, Psort 22.62 to 21.72; steps
  equal.  Profile of QueensSet9 after the change: the time above the old
  run is in node_reserve after a sweep (the free lists hand out cold
  blocks), in the sweeps, and in the destruction of about a million
  configurations of dead queues.  Tests: unit_cxx_gc.py (TestOwnership: a
  sort by permutation leaves nothing, set functions consumed in part are
  freed during a run, the configuration trigger, nested set functions, an
  error inside a set function, the split; TestSplitValues on both
  backends; test_growth), unit_cxx_variable.py (SmallVec resize).  Docs:
  the environment variables, the benchmark README.  Left: QueensSet9 costs
  11 percent more CPU with the collector on than before, the price of
  freeing memory with this collector (the block heap of the memory note is
  the fix); the Python backend still shares one configuration object
  between the two queues of a split; a collection in a Python callback
  still treats every older node as a root; a Queue still holds its
  configurations in a std::deque, about 600 bytes even when empty.
- Collector stress mode (G2, issue #26).  The collector of the C++ backend
  ran once per 2^20 nodes, so a missing root showed up late or never.  Done:
  with SPRITE_GC_STRESS=1 the request flag stays set (the library sets it
  when it loads, and run_gc sets it again), so check_interrupts returns E_GC
  after every rewrite step and the scheduler collects at every safepoint it
  reaches; a nested set function hands the request outward as before.  The
  hot path is unchanged.  gc_stress() in the bindings reports the mode,
  gc_collections() counts.  The mark stack is kept between collections.  The
  value 0 or an empty value is off; another value is off with a warning.
  Found on the way, on the first run of the unit suite in stress mode: (1)
  hnf_or_free left the residual that its probe of a free variable recorded
  (instantiate adds one) in the configuration, so a step interrupted after
  the rewrite of =:<= returned the nested scheduler to ready() with a void
  residual, and queens of CxxGc.curry ended in a false suspension; a normal
  run meets the same when the periodic rotation lands there, once in 65536
  steps.  hnf_or_free takes the residual back now.  (2) applygnf ($##)
  reported the free variables left in an interrupted normalization, or in a
  constraint that hnf lifted to the root, as residuals, and the
  configuration suspended for good: id $## (let x free in (x =:= 1) &> x)
  suspended in a normal run as well.  The ground check applies only when the
  normalization completed and the redex became the application.  (3) The
  scheduler rotated the queue after every collection (rotate(Q, true)); in
  stress mode that turned a depth-first search into a lockstep over all
  alternatives (the sudoku and cryptarithm examples never finished) and
  starved the sibling in a nested queue (nestedChoice of StackGuard.curry).
  A collection changes nothing in the schedule now: the interrupted
  configuration continues, with its forced flag so that ready() accepts it,
  and check_interrupts counts every compression and checks the rotation
  before the request.  So the stress mode follows the schedule of a normal
  run: the test programs give equal values, in the same order, with equal
  step counts.  A test whose child cannot end in stress mode skips there
  (cytest.skipIfGcStress: the out-of-memory program, the 200000-element
  value, the deep recursion, the threshold and growth policy tests;
  unit_examples leaves out the two search examples).  Measured: Tak0 in
  stress mode takes 22.7 s against 0.72 s, with 9350060 collections for
  9350060 steps and a peak RSS of 35 MB against 122 MB; the throughput suite
  is unchanged (ratios 0.94 to 1.08, counters equal).  The unit suite in
  stress mode on this machine: 46 files green, one process per file, about 5
  minutes warm.  CI: the job stress of ci.yml runs the unit suite on the C++
  backend in stress mode on the nightly schedule and on request (input
  stress); the push jobs are unchanged.  Tests: unit_cxx_gc.py (TestStress:
  collections per step, the same values and steps as without the mode, a
  nested callback evaluation, off unless asked; the base class ChildTests,
  so the inherited tests run once), unit_examples.py (STRESS_EXCLUDED),
  unit_cxx_stack.py and unit_cxx_runtime.py (the skips).  Docs: the
  environment variables, tests/README.  Left: the Python backend has the
  same probe in hnf_or_free (fairscheme.py) and no stress mode; test_convert
  of unit_curry2icurry fails whenever the ICurry cache holds its modules
  (the front end does not run, so no .fcy file appears), with or without the
  stress mode; a nested scheduler that hands E_ROTATE or E_UNWIND outward
  from a queue of one configuration leaves the front without the forced
  flag.

2026-10-04
- Scheduler counters (M4, issue #10).  The parallelism gate asks how much of
  the work of a program the queue could run in parallel, and nothing
  measured it.  Done: make COUNTERS=1 adds -DSPRITE_SCHEDULER_COUNTERS (like
  TRACE=1; the objects do not depend on the variable, so touch Make.config
  to switch).  The runtime then counts per evaluation (state/counters.hpp):
  the steps taken while the outermost queue held one configuration, the
  steps inside set functions, the steps whose redex another configuration
  created, the largest outermost queue, and the lifetime of every
  configuration that ends by a value, a failure, or a fork (a histogram,
  exact below 1024 steps, one bucket per power of two above), for the
  outermost queue and for the nested ones.  The creator of a node is the
  serial number of the configuration whose step allocated it; node_reserve
  writes it into the word before the node, and procS compares it with the
  stepping configuration.  So "shared" is work on a node that an ancestor
  made before a fork or that a sibling made: an upper bound of what a design
  with one copy of the graph per alternative would duplicate.  The flag
  changes the runtime and the bindings only: the new fields are last in
  RuntimeState and Configuration, node_reserve is out of line, and
  generated code reads neither, so the staged modules serve both builds.
  The bindings hand the raw counters to Python (RuntimeState.
  scheduler_counters, scheduler_counters_enabled); EvaluationTotals sums
  them over the evaluations (the maxima take the largest), and curry.stats
  and sprite-exec --stats append fourteen keys after compile (stats.
  SCHEDULER_KEYS: serial_steps, nested_steps, shared_steps, queue_max,
  configurations, failures, failed_steps, lifetime_median, lifetime_mean,
  lifetime_max, and the nested four).  The harness accepts the longer line
  and keeps it in extra.stats; run_benchmarks counters FILE prints one row
  per program with the serial, nested, shared, and failed fractions.  On
  the way: Queue keeps its size in a counter (the size of a deque is an
  arithmetic over its iterators, and count_step asks at every step), and
  single_step of the bindings steps the configuration the evaluator made
  instead of a second one for the same root.  Measured, the plain and the
  instrumented runtime side by side under perf stat (the machine had
  another load; instructions are the stable number): Tak1 26.39 to 27.23 G
  instructions (+3.2 percent), cycles +6 to +8 percent; PermSort 29.68 to
  30.24 G (+1.9), cycles +3 to +5; QueensSet 9.21 to 9.30 G (+1.0), cycles
  within the noise; peak RSS +8 to +20 percent (the word per node).  The
  5 percent budget holds for PermSort and QueensSet in cycles and for all
  three in instructions; Tak1, which allocates most, misses it by the
  cache misses of the larger heap.  The 30 dissertation programs (one
  repetition each, the file in the measure directory of the build tree):
  the sixteen programs without a fork have a serial fraction of 1 and one
  configuration; twelve search programs have a serial fraction below 0.07
  (Half, SearchMAC, ColormapChoice, ColormapFree, Horseman, Last,
  PaliFunPats, PermSort, PermSortPeano, QueensSet, RegExp, SearchQueens),
  a median lifetime of 0 to 8 steps (mean 2 to 33), and a shared-work ratio
  of 0.10 (PermSortPeano) to 0.99 (ColormapChoice); QueensSet runs 97
  percent of its steps inside set functions.  PokerChoice and PokerFree do
  not compile here (the front end wants currypp), as in the baseline.
  Tests: unit_cxx_counters.py (the median and the sum in Python on every
  backend; on an instrumented runtime the exact counters of count, coinNot,
  oneFails, twoValues, inSet of data/curry/CxxCounters.curry and of psort
  and countQueens of CxxGc.curry, the invariant that every step belongs to
  one configuration of the outermost queue, a configuration left in the
  queue, a single step, the sum over evaluations, the sprite-exec line; on
  a plain runtime the seven fields alone), unit_api.py (the keys after the
  seven), unit_benchmarks.py (the parser, the counters command).  Docs: the
  sprite-exec page, tests/README, the benchmark README.  Left: the gate
  reading: the search programs have the parallelism in the queue but each
  configuration lives a handful of steps and most of its steps are on
  shared nodes, so a worker must own a subtree and the graph must stay
  shared; the shared counter tags every node, so a build that wants the
  serial fraction and the lifetimes alone pays for the word too (a second
  level of the flag would split it); the nested lifetimes miss the
  configurations still in the queues of set functions, which hang off
  SetEval nodes.
- Plain-data module format (L5, issue #18).  With the precompiled header a
  small module still cost g++ about 0.3 s for its static Metadata maps
  (std::unordered_map of std::variant) and its ModuleBOM aggregate
  (std::string, std::vector, std::map, std::tuple): the instantiation of the
  templates and the code of the dynamic initializers.  Done: the generated
  code spells the bill of materials as static arrays of plain records (new
  header cyrt/bom.hpp, namespace cyrt::bom: Entry, Metadata, Alias, Type,
  Function, Module; every member is an integer, a pointer, or a string
  literal) and exports one record, _bom_.  The record is declared extern
  inside the extern "C" block, because a const object at namespace scope has
  internal linkage otherwise.  The compiler writes the arrays as data: no
  template is instantiated for them and no constructor runs at load.  The
  loader (SharedCurryModule) reads the record, refuses one of another layout
  version (bom::VERSION; the DynloadError names both versions; a library
  without the record and a record without a name are errors too), and
  decodes it into the ModuleBOM the runtime and the bindings read
  (ModuleBOM::decode).  The registry entry owns the decoded object.  A
  metadata record shared between symbols is decoded once (the Prelude shares
  36 records among about 1,700 symbols).  Module::link, loader.py, and the
  Python view of the bill of materials are unchanged.  FORMAT_VERSION 3 -> 4:
  a format-3 file no longer compiles against the new headers (the first
  member of ModuleBOM is now the deque of the decoded metadata), and the
  stamp keeps it from the compiler.  The ABI stamp is unchanged; the header
  change recompiles every object once.  The emitter refuses a metadata value
  that is not a str, an int, or a bool, and an int outside the C++ range,
  with a CompileError that names the key (g++ failed on such a value
  before).  The symbol kind MODULE_DATA ('T') names the tables.  Measured on
  the C++ backend: g++ on Fib.cpp with the precompiled header 0.62 -> 0.35 s
  user (0.73 -> 0.44 s wall); -ftime-report: opt and generate 0.55 -> 0.27 s,
  template instantiation 0.20 -> 0.15 s.  Peano takes 0.54 s user (0.64 s
  wall) after the change: its 15 step functions cost 0.47 s in opt and
  generate at -O3.  The Prelude 27.4 -> 26.5 s user (its 3,194 step
  functions take almost all of it).  Compile suite (-r 3, CPU seconds of the
  whole process): Peano warm 1.230 -> 0.855 (0.70), Fib warm 0.918 -> 0.642
  (0.70), cold 0.83 and 0.79; the compile field: Peano warm 1.103 -> 0.737
  (0.67), Fib warm 0.793 -> 0.530 (0.67).  func_eqconstr.py on the C++
  backend with every module regenerated and compiled again (767 programs;
  CPU seconds of the test process and its children; five chunks of the
  file, one process each): 569.5 -> 318.8 s (0.56).  Throughput (seven
  programs, -r 3): every item the same within 3 percent, counters equal;
  the import suite the same.  Static evidence: the module objects (the test
  module and the staged Prelude.so) import no operator new or delete, no
  _Hashtable, and no basic_string; before, every module imported operator
  new for its maps.  Tests: unit_cxx_bom.py (the decoded bill of materials
  of data/curry/CxxBom.curry, with a metadata value of each kind and a
  private function; the generated file; the undefined symbols of the
  objects, the Prelude included; the loader's checks on hand-compiled
  libraries; the emitter's errors).  Left: g++ now spends its time on the
  step functions (code generation at -O3 and the instantiation of the inline
  indexing, about 0.15 s per module), so a module of Peano's size stays
  above 0.4 s; the InfoTable constructor is out of line, so every static
  info table is initialized by a call at load (1,641 calls for the Prelude);
  an aggregate InfoTable would make the tables data as well; the C++
  DynloadError reaches Python as RuntimeError, not as
  curry.exceptions.DynloadError.
- Review of the throughput stream: the set guard at the root and the
  interrupted step (issues #20, #21, #25, #26 follow-up).  (1) A set function
  whose value is a sub-term of its guarded argument (set1 id x, set1 anyOf
  xs, set1 tails xs, set1 (\xs -> head xs ? last xs); the defect of
  2026-10-03) put the guard of the argument at the root of the nested
  configuration, or at the inductive position of a step after a rewrite.
  procD and hnf said assert(0); continue there: the debug flavor aborted,
  the release flavor of T1 looped (isEmpty (set1 id 1) spun until the cap
  killed it).  Done: procD treats a guard at the root as a node of the
  spine, like a constructor.  procN descends into it, and a choice below it
  is pulled up through it, which puts the choice into the escape set of the
  guard's set (Scan::copy_spine), so the choice escapes the set function;
  make_value drops the guards of the set from the value.  hnf crosses a
  guard at the inductive position as the indexer does (Variable::skip): the
  set joins the guards of the variable and the guarded expression becomes
  the target, so rvalue puts the guard back and a choice below it joins the
  escape set.  allValues_step accepted only a constructor as a value and
  took anything else for the escaping choice; a free variable, and a term
  under the guard of an enclosing set function (the inner of two nested set
  functions returns its argument, which carries both guards), are values
  too.  The C++ backend gives the values of the Python backend for every
  shape of the defect, and two values, [[1]] and [[2]], for sortValues
  (set1 inner (1 ? 2)) with inner x = sortValues (set1 id x), where the
  Python backend fails an assertion (allValues expects a choice when D
  unwinds for a guard of another set).  (2) hnf_or_free took back the
  residual of the variable its probe recorded, but not that of the
  variable's group (add_residual records both): after y =:= x, the probe of
  x in x =:<= e left the group's residual in the configuration until the
  constraint reached the root.  And a nested scheduler that yielded E_ROTATE
  or E_UNWIND from a queue of one configuration set no forced flag (the
  E_GC path did).  Together, a periodic rotation or the stack limit inside
  such a step made the nested ready() find one void residual and throw
  EvaluationSuspended for the whole goal.  Reproduced on the C++ backend: a
  set function of one configuration beside a long alternative, the
  constraint under 3000 strict applications (the window of the
  interruption), and a preamble whose length puts the 65536th forward node
  inside the window (two of 55 lengths scanned suspended); and a stack
  limit of 64 KiB inside x =:= deep (a suspension instead of the stack
  error).  Done: Configuration::remove_residual erases the variable and its
  group, and both yields set the forced flag, as E_GC does.  Found on the
  way: in a deterministic run the nested scheduler calls ready() once and
  then not before an interrupt or a choice, so the residuals of completed
  steps pile up in the configuration until then; they are bound by then
  and hide a void one, which is why the suspension needs the interrupt in
  the first such step after a ready().  Measured with the harness (C++
  backend, three repetitions, CPU seconds, before to after; steps, forks,
  and peak RSS equal): Tak1 2.57 to 2.59 (1.01), Fib 0.73 to 0.76 (1.05),
  PermSort 7.92 to 8.35 (1.05), Queens10 3.84 to 3.94 (1.03); the changes
  are off the hot path (a case label, a rare branch, and two erases), and
  under perf stat the run after takes 24.49 G instructions for Tak1, 4.81 G
  for Fib, and 24.24 G for PermSort, below the counts of the M4 entry
  (measured under another load).  Against the
  baseline of 2026-10-03: Tak1 6.87 to 2.59, Fib 1.06 to 0.76, PermSort
  13.22 to 8.35 (peak RSS 3181 MB to 490 MB), Queens10 6.62 to 3.94,
  QueensSet 1.30 to 1.11 (578 MB to 145 MB; steps 2172616 to 2152081,
  forks 511344 to 511413, see the G1 entry).  Tests: unit_cxx_scheduler.py
  with data/curry/CxxScheduler.curry (TestSetGuardRoot on both backends:
  the values of the shapes above, the escaping choice of the argument, a
  free variable as the argument; TestNestedSetGuard: the nested set
  functions, and the captured argument as an expected failure;
  TestNestedSetGuardPython: the nested case as an expected failure of the
  Python backend; TestInterruptedStep: the stack limit and the rotation
  inside the constraint, the latter a scan of 55 preamble lengths over one
  rotation period, skipped in the stress mode of the collector).  Without
  the fixes the two interruption tests fail with EvaluationSuspended, and
  the set-guard children time out.  Left: a choice of the argument of an
  enclosing set function that the inner function captures (not receives)
  carries the outer guard only, so the inner set function encapsulates it,
